| fold(
  "FIX-DEDUPCHECK-MATCHEDTASKID-UNANCHORED-SUBSTRING-MISLABEL";
  "\n\n[po/triage 2026-08-26T03:48Z] FOLD +1 occurrence AND +1 MANDATORY REPLAY TARGET. Envelope 2c3cd107d (router, detector_defect, MED) reports the SAME root cause this row names -- DATA-tier dedup matches on TABLE NAME with no discrimination of what KIND of row it matched -- with a second live masking consequence. Occurrence: signal_outcomes STALE/HIGH (outcome evaluation stalled 31 days) was deduped into ALPHA-S5-TRUTH-LEDGER-BACKTEST, a BACKLOG feature row about building backtest calibration ON TOP OF signal_outcomes, next_agent unset, untouched since 2026-07-14. The match is pure table-name collision: the epic's title mentions signal_outcomes as a data SOURCE, it is not a row that could ever fix the stall. Net effect: a HIGH defect finding is now suppressed under an unowned P2 FEATURE epic and has no owner and no actionable row anywhere.\nThis is the SECOND finding confirmed masked by this defect (the first, macro_indicators STALE -> FIX-ORCHSTATE-TASKBOARD-HEAD-REINFLATION-GUARD, is already in this row's 2026-08-14 SCOPE ADDITION). The replay list this row's own AC already requires is therefore: (1) macro_indicators staleness, (2) signal_outcomes 31-day outcome-evaluation stall. Fixing the matcher without replaying BOTH leaves two live data-integrity findings invisible.\nADDITIONAL DISCRIMINATOR the fix should carry, beyond word-boundary/exact-field matching: a matched row whose next_agent is unset AND whose lane is backlog AND whose class is FEATURE cannot legitimately absorb a defect finding -- an unowned feature epic is not a tracking row. Both false matches so far would have been rejected by that predicate alone.\nCROSS-REF: the signal_outcomes stall itself has a partially-covering row, FIX-SIGNAL-OUTCOMES-LIVENESS-BLIND-TO-ZERO-PRODUCTION (backlog, owner=po, next_agent UNSET). That row's own next_agent gap is why the finding had nowhere correct to land even absent this defect; it needs an owner assigned independently of this fix."
)
| fold(
  "SPIKE-SESSION-PRESENCE-ROSTER-UNDERCOUNTS-LIVE-SESSIONS";
  "\n\n[po/triage 2026-08-26T03:48Z] FOLD -- BLAST-RADIUS ADDITION, no new row, and explicitly NOT a reopen of the Step 2.4 Axis C row (that row passed QA and is DONE_VERIFIED; the finding below is a hazard introduced by a sanctioned design, not an AC failure). Envelope 847066d1d (router, system-issue, WARN).\nA NEW CONSUMER now depends on this broken oracle in its most dangerous direction. Step 2.4 Axis C (landed 4f72c694c) promotes the Phase A.5 presence roster -- a read its OWN SKILL.md labels 'advisory; non-blocking' -- into a PERMISSIVE dispatch gate: a current-period cowork-slot marker whose owner_client_session is ABSENT from the roster is skipped as 'abandoned, not live', allowing the dispatch to proceed. Presence-absence is exactly the ambiguity this SPIKE exists to measure (a live session between presence renewals reads identical to a dead one), and here the ambiguity resolves in the direction whose failure mode is a DOUBLE PUBLISH to a real channel.\nWHY THIS RAISES THIS SPIKE'S VALUE: until now the roster's undercount produced conservative errors (a live session read as absent merely lost a claim). Axis C inverts the sign -- the same undercount now GRANTS a dispatch. The SPIKE's output must therefore include a directional statement, not just a count: for each consumer, does roster-absence fail safe or fail open. Two known fail-open consumers today: Step 2.4 Axis C, and FIX-CRON-REARM-STEP1B1-LIVENESS-ORACLE-BLIND-WINDOW-FALSE-LIVE (ready[], developer).\nCross-ref memory: feedback_step24_cowork_collision_probe_ttl_exceeds_cadence_daily_false_positive and feedback_router_almost_rearmed_live_peer_cron_markers_20260822 both record that heartbeat AND presence lie for one renewal cadence after a close."
)
