    "owner": "po",
    "next_agent": "architect",
    "status": "BACKLOG",
    "priority": "P0",
    "zone": "scripts/",
    "created_at": "2026-08-26T03:48:12Z",
    "dedup_key": "qadrain:done-to-qa-backward-lane-rank|conservation-abort-deadlock",
    "origin_signal_id": "rtr-20260826T0325-qadrain-conservation-deadlock",
    "files": [
      "scripts/devteam-review-claim-qa-drain.jq",
      "scripts/orch-conservation-check.mjs"
    ],
    "status_note": "MINTED by po triage 2026-08-26T03:48Z from router system-issue envelope d5eabd0c0 (CRITICAL). MECHANISM INDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED IN-FILE THIS TICK, not taken on the envelope's word: scripts/devteam-review-claim-qa-drain.jq's own selection comment states 'candidate lanes: .task_board.review[] UNION .task_board.done[]', a DELIBERATE widening per architect brief docs/architecture-briefs/2026-08-08-donelane-doneverified-producer.md (done[] was a terminal-LOOKING lane with zero consumers, so nothing produced the DONE_VERIFIED token deps_satisfied() requires). scripts/orch-conservation-check.mjs:216 declares FLAT_TASK_LANES = [backlog,ready,in_progress,review,qa,done,done_verified] and :378 derives LANE_RANK straight from that array order, so done=5 and qa=4 and :430 scores done->qa as BACKWARD. :498 reads MAX_UNDECLARED_BACKWARD_MOVES default 1 and :500 aborts above it.\nTWO SHIPPED COMPONENTS DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT 'done' MEANS -- neither is individually wrong, the LANE VOCABULARY is. This board uses done[] = developer-complete AWAITING QA and done_verified[] = post-QA terminal (evidence: the blocked rows carry status=DONE AND next_agent=qa; a row still naming qa as next agent is by definition not terminal). LANE_RANK is a single FLAT array reused for a board with TWO terminal lanes and a NON-LINEAR DONE->QA->DONE_VERIFIED path.\nMEASURED LIVE 2026-08-26T03:22Z: review[] eligible (status=REVIEW,next=qa)=2, done[] eligible (status=DONE,next=qa)=2 -- exceeding the tolerance of 1 by exactly one row. First attempt aborted with '[orch-conservation-check] ABORTED -- lane-placement violation: 2 task row(s) moved BACKWARD ... exceeding the tolerated 1 undeclared backward move(s)'; rows drained on that attempt = 0 of 4 eligible, INCLUDING 3 P0. Both done[]-origin rows carried updated_at=2026-08-26T01:23:29Z, the same stamp -- one batch write earlier that night created the deadlock, which is why the lane drained normally before that day and why the defect only became observable then.\nWHY DEADLOCK AND NOT THROTTLE: a done[]-origin row leaves done[] ONLY by being claimed into qa[] by this same script. While >=2 sit there every invocation aborts, so none is ever claimed, so the count never falls back to 1. Stable at >=2 forever without outside intervention. Blast radius is the whole batch -- strictly-forward review[]-origin rows are discarded along with it, so a lane holding 27 review rows drains ZERO.\nCURRENT STATE (checked at mint time): done[] holds 6 rows and NONE currently carries next_agent=qa, so the deadlock is DISARMED right now. It re-arms the moment two done rows are flipped to next_agent=qa. Do not read the present green as a fix.\nWORKAROUND ALREADY USED, EXPLICITLY NOT A FIX: the 03:22Z router re-ran with ORCH_APPLY_DECLARED_BACKWARD_LANE_MOVES set to exactly the two ids (the hatch the guard's own abort message documents), scoped after diffing candidate-vs-live across all seven lanes to confirm nothing else moved non-forward; the batch of 4 then landed (3 P0 + 1 P1), review 29->27, qa 3->7, done 8->6. A router hand-setting a safety-bypass env var every time the lane is selected is not durable -- the next tick, and every peer session, hits the same wall with no memory of the analysis.\nAC: (1) two or more done[]-origin rows with next_agent=qa are claimable by scripts/devteam-review-claim-qa-drain.jq in a SINGLE orch-apply write with NO caller-set ORCH_APPLY_DECLARED_BACKWARD_LANE_MOVES and no raised tolerance; (2) a genuine stale-full-doc revert of >=2 rows STILL aborts (negative control -- prove the guard is not merely disarmed); (3) a fixture replays the exact 03:22Z shape (2 review + 2 done eligible) and lands 4 rows.\nFIX DIRECTION -- architect's call, three shapes ranked by the reporter: (1) teach the lane-placement dimension that done->qa is legitimate when the row carries next_agent==\"qa\", i.e. exempt exactly the transition the qa-drain script is designed to make -- narrowest, keeps the guard fully armed against real stale-revert writes, no vocabulary change; (2) have devteam-review-claim-qa-drain.jq emit the declaration itself for its own done[]-origin picks so no caller ever needs the env var -- localizes the knowledge in the component that holds the intent; (3) split the lane vocabulary so the rank array stops conflating 'developer done' with 'verified done' -- correct but widest blast radius, every consumer of FLAT_TASK_LANES.\nHARD FORBID: do NOT resolve this by raising CONSERVATION_MAX_UNDECLARED_BACKWARD_MOVES. That tolerance is what catches genuine stale-full-doc reverts (see memory feedback_orchapply_cas_window_misses_caller_read_transform_gap); raising it to cover N done-origin rows disarms it for exactly the class it exists to catch.\nRELATED, NOT DUPLICATE: memory feedback_caller_readback_lane_narrower_than_claim_script_silently_kills_lane records the SECONDARY drain dying on the SAME review-union-done design -- there the CALLER's readback was narrower than the script's selector; here the CONSERVATION GUARD rejects the union's done-half outright. Same union, two consumers, two different silent-death mechanisms. Worth scoping together. Also related and distinct: FIX-QADRAIN-NO-TASKID-LEVEL-CLAIM-DUPLICATE-QA-SPAWN (duplicate spawn, not conservation) and FIX-DONELANE-DEVTEAM-FLOWDOC-PROSE-SYNC (prose sync for the same done[] widening)."
  },
  {
    "id": "FIX-AUDITOR-DATATIER-DURABLE-TRAIL-NARRATED-NUMBERS-CONTRADICT-OWN-EVIDENCE",
    "title": "system-auditor AUDIT_TIER=DATA writes LLM-NARRATED numbers into its durable trail instead of computed ones, and they are wrong in a severity-REDUCING direction -- elapsed-age deltas understated 15.6x and 2.8x in records that state the correct raw timestamp in the same sentence, plus a dedup_skipped counter emitted as 0 while the same return's own history entry records 3 and its prose says 2",
    "owner": "po",
    "next_agent": "developer",
    "status": "BACKLOG",
    "priority": "P1",
    "zone": "docs/agents/system-auditor/flow/",
    "created_at": "2026-08-26T03:48:12Z",
    "dedup_key": "datatier:durable-trail-narrated-numbers-contradict-measured",
    "origin_signal_id": "rtr-20260826T0234-auditordedupskipzero",
    "files": [
      "docs/agents/system-auditor/flow/main.md",
      "scripts/db-integrity-history-append.sh"
    ],
    "status_note": "MINTED by po triage 2026-08-26T03:48Z from two router detector_defect envelopes, 8dbffd56a (WARN) and d7a5e9153 (WARN). Filed as ONE row with TWO arms because both are the same failure mode -- a number that a model NARRATED being written into the durable audit trail in place of a number that was MEASURED -- and both are fixed by the same shape.\nARM A -- ELAPSED-TIME DELTAS, WRONG IN A SEVERITY-REDUCING DIRECTION. Scan 2026-08-26T03:30:22Z: the signal_outcomes detail says 'no outcome checks in 2+ days' when the real age is 31.14 days (15.6x understated); the macro_indicators detail says 'approximately 14+ hours old' when the real age is 39.3 hours (2.8x understated). BOTH details state the CORRECT raw timestamp and then contradict it in the same sentence, so each record falsifies itself on its face and is checkable with no external data. The direction matters more than the magnitude: every error so far makes a finding look FRESHER, i.e. less severe, than it is -- a 31-day stall narrated as '2+ days' will not be prioritised as a stall.\nARM B -- DEDUP_SKIPPED COUNTER. The [OUTPUT-CONTRACT] emission on the 2026-08-26T02:30:33Z run reported dedup_skipped=0 while that same return's OWN history entry records 3 findings with dedup_check.already_open=true and its OWN prose narration says 2 -- THREE different values for one quantity inside a single return. Not a one-off: the prior DATA entry (scan_ts 2026-08-25T22:35:28Z) likewise records 2 already_open findings against an emitted 0.\nWHY ARM B IS LOAD-BEARING AND NOT COSMETIC: dedup_skipped is the only number that makes suppression VISIBLE in the trail. Reporting 0 while 3 findings were suppressed is exactly what let the two confirmed false-suppression incidents tracked on FIX-DEDUPCHECK-MATCHEDTASKID-UNANCHORED-SUBSTRING-MISLABEL (macro_indicators, and signal_outcomes -> ALPHA-S5-TRUTH-LEDGER-BACKTEST) stay invisible. Fixing the matcher there without fixing the counter here leaves the next false suppression equally unobservable.\nAC: (1) every numeric field in a DATA-tier durable record is COMPUTED from the raw timestamp/count it cites, never authored in prose -- ages derived by arithmetic on the stated timestamp; (2) a pre-write self-consistency assert fails loud when an emitted counter disagrees with the count of records it summarises (this run would have failed on 0 vs 3); (3) replay the 2026-08-26T03:30:22Z and 2026-08-25T22:35:28Z entries and show corrected values; (4) NEGATIVE CONTROL -- a fixture whose narrated and measured values agree must still PASS, proving the assert is not vacuous.\nNOT A DUPLICATE OF FIX-AUDIT-OUTPUT-CONTRACT-V4-V5-DEDUPSKIP-DENOMINATOR-FALSE-VIOLATION: that row is about V4/V5 COMPARING a NEW-only anomaly tally against an ALL-emissions signals_posted counter (a comparison-denominator defect that yields false VIOLATION reports). This row is about the dedup_skipped VALUE ITSELF being emitted as 0 when the emitter's own records say 3 -- an upstream producer defect. Same accounting seam, different half; the V4/V5 comparison cannot be made correct while one of its inputs is fabricated. Cross-reference, do not merge.\nALSO DISTINCT FROM: FIX-AUDITOR-DATATIER-CRONJOBRUNS-ADHOC-COUNT-NONDETERMINISTIC (hand-authored query per run) and FIX-AUDITOR-DATATIER-REINVOKES-STATEFUL-PREGATE-SELF-DEFEATS-ROUTER-SPAWN (stateful probe re-invocation)."
  },
